1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,920
The athlete who comes second is measurably less happy than the athlete who comes third.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,920
Not a feeling. Not sometimes.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,920
A number, pulled off the faces of Olympic medalists the moment they finished.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,920
You have an explanation ready. Silver is a sore loser.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,920
But that is not it. What you are watching is counterfactual thinking, and the same machine is running in you tonight.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,920
Your mind never scores your life against your life.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,920
It scores it against an invented one standing right beside it.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,920
By the end you will know why the near miss hurts worse than the far one, where that came from,

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,920
and the one question that gets the invented rival out of the room.

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,920
Here is how anyone found this out in the first place.

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,920
In 1995, three psychologists named Victoria Medvec, Scott Madey and Thomas Gilovich

12
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,920
took the television footage of the Barcelona Olympics and did something almost sneaky with it.

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,920
They cut the sound. They stripped out the commentary, the flags, the scoreboards, anything that would tell you who had won what.

14
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,920
Then they showed those silent faces to people who knew nothing about the events and asked one question.

15
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:11,920
How does this person feel, on a scale that runs from agony at one end to ecstasy at the other?

16
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,920
The raters had no idea they were sorting medals.

17
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,920
They were just reading faces. And the faces came out in the wrong order.

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,920
At the moment of finishing, the bronze medalists scored around seven out of ten.

19
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,920
The silver medalists scored under five.

20
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,920
The person who had just been beaten by almost everyone in the final looked happier than the person who had beaten everyone but one.

21
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,920
Objectively, silver is the better afternoon. Silver is closer to the top of the world than any human being alive except one.

22
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,920
And silver looked like grief. Then the researchers listened to what the athletes actually said afterward,

23
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,920
and the reason walked straight out of their own mouths.

24
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,920
The silver medalists talked in almosts.

25
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,920
I almost had it. Another tenth of a second.

26
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,920
If the turn had been cleaner. The bronze medalists talked in at leasts.

27
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:01,920
At least I medalled. At least I am on this podium at all.

28
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,920
Same podium, same day, same sport, and two completely different invisible worlds being used as the measuring stick.

29
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:15,920
Silver is standing next to gold. Bronze is standing next to fourth place, and fourth place is a name nobody will ever say again.

30
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:20,920
That is counterfactual thinking, and once you can see it you cannot unsee it.

31
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,920
Your mind does not evaluate what happened. It builds the nearest version of what almost happened, sets it down next to reality,

32
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,920
and reports the difference to you as an emotion.

33
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,920
The closer the invented world sits, the louder the report.

34
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,920
Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky demonstrated this with a situation so ordinary it barely sounds like an experiment.

35
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,920
Two travellers are stuck in the same traffic, arrive at the same airport at the same minute, and both miss their flights.

36
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,920
One missed his by half an hour. The other missed his by five minutes.

37
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,920
Which man is more upset? Everybody knows the answer instantly, and that is the point.

38
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,920
Roughly ninety six out of a hundred people said the man who missed by five minutes.

39
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,920
But look at what actually happened to each of them. Identical outcome.

40
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,920
Same night in the same airport, same missed meeting, same hotel.

41
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,920
Nothing in the real world separates them at all.

42
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:16,920
The only thing that differs is how easy it is to imagine the other version, the one where the traffic broke a little sooner.

43
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,920
Five minutes is a world you can almost touch.

44
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,920
Half an hour is not. And your mind charges you emotionally for the distance to the world you can almost touch, not for anything that occurred.

45
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,920
So the near miss is not a bigger loss.

46
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,920
It is a smaller gap to an imaginary place. Which raises an uncomfortable question about the way you have been keeping score your whole life,

47
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,920
because you are running this on far more than medals and flights.

48
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,920
The job that came down to two candidates hurts more than the fifty that never replied.

49
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,920
The message that got read and not answered hurts more than silence.

50
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,920
The relationship that nearly worked haunts you in a way the ones that obviously never could do not.

51
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,920
In every case the pain is not tracking the size of what you lost.

52
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,920
It is tracking how vividly you can picture the version where it went the other way.

53
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,920
And before you decide your brain is broken, understand what the psychologist Neal Roese spent years demonstrating.

54
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,920
Counterfactual thinking is not a malfunction.

55
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,920
It is preparation. When your mind generates the if only, it is not torturing you for entertainment,

56
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,920
it is extracting a lesson and pointing it forward.

57
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,920
People who run those upward comparisons after failing actually perform better on the next attempt.

58
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:28,920
The sting is the delivery mechanism. It hurts precisely so that you remember, and it hurts most for the gaps that were closeable,

59
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,920
because those are the only ones worth remembering.

60
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:37,920
Which is exactly where you have to leave the laboratory, because a machine that punishes you for near misses

61
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,920
only makes sense in a world that no longer exists.

62
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,920
Go back two hundred thousand years.

63
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:50,920
You are living in a band of maybe thirty people, and you will spend your whole life inside it.

64
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,920
Every person you will ever be compared to is standing within shouting distance right now.

65
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,920
There is no ladder stretching up out of sight. There is no next level.

66
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,920
There are about thirty faces, and your position among them is the entire scoreboard.

67
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,920
Now notice something strange about that world.

68
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,920
In it, only one comparison ever carried information.

69
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,920
The person immediately ahead of you. Not the legendary hunter three camps over that you would never meet,

70
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:25,920
not some abstract best in the world, but the one specific person standing between you and a better share of the meat,

71
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,920
a place nearer the fire, the mate you wanted.

72
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,920
That gap was small enough to close.

73
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:38,920
Watch what they did, do it slightly better next season, and you moved.

74
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:45,920
Meanwhile the gap to somebody far above you carried nothing at all, because no amount of effort this year was going to cross it.

75
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,920
A brain that felt the near gap as agony and the far gap as a shrug was not being cruel.

76
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,920
It was spending your attention on the only distance you could actually walk.

77
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,920
The stakes underneath that were not pride.

78
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,920
The anthropologist James Woodburn studied societies that live hand to mouth,

79
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,920
taking what the land gives and consuming it quickly, and he documented how ferociously

80
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,920
flat those groups stay.

81
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,920
Nobody is allowed to accumulate. Nobody is allowed to rise.

82
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:21,920
Equality there is not a gentle value, it is enforced daily, because a group where one person can hoard

83
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,920
status and food is a group that gets somebody killed.

84
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,920
Richard Lee, who lived for years among the Ju/'hoansi of the Kalahari,

85
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,920
ran into that enforcement personally.

86
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:41,920
For Christmas he bought the camp an enormous ox, the biggest animal he could find, expecting delight.

87
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,920
Instead everyone told him it was a bag of bones.

88
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,920
Skin and ribs. Barely worth the walk.

89
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,920
He was baffled and slightly hurt until it was explained to him plainly.

90
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:59,920
When a man kills a great animal, he starts to think of himself as a big man, and a big man eventually gets someone hurt.

91
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:05,920
So the camp mocks the meat. They cool his heart while he is still young, and they keep him gentle.

92
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:10,920
Sit with what that means. Your ancestors did not merely live without a status ladder.

93
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:16,920
They actively dismantled the top rung, every time one started to form, on purpose,

94
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:22,920
for two hundred thousand years. The scoreboard was small, it was local, it was everyone you knew,

95
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,920
and the group itself worked to keep the distances short.

96
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:30,920
Your near miss machine was built for that. Thirty rivals, closeable gaps,

97
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:34,920
and a rematch next season with the same people.

98
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:38,920
Now open your phone. The band is not thirty people anymore.

99
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:45,920
It is everyone. Your comparison set has no ceiling and no floor and it refreshes every time your thumb moves.

100
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,920
And the machine inside you does not know that.

101
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:54,920
It is still doing the only job it has ever done, scanning for the person just ahead of you, the one whose gap looks closeable,

102
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:00,920
and reporting the distance as pain. Except now there is an infinite supply of them,

103
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:07,920
sorted and delivered by a feed, each one carefully cropped to look like a life you were about six inches away from having.

104
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:13,920
That is the mismatch. Not that you compare yourself to others, your ancestors did that constantly

105
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,920
and it kept them alive.

106
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:23,920
It is that the near miss used to be rare, local, and followed by a rematch, and now it is endless,

107
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,920
global, and followed by nothing.

108
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:33,920
You lose to a stranger in another country whose actual life you cannot see, on a scoreboard nobody agreed to,

109
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:39,920
with no next season. The silver medalist at least gets another Games.

110
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,920
You are being handed a silver medal forty times before lunch.

111
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,920
So here is the shift, and it is smaller than it sounds.

112
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,920
You cannot stop your mind building the other world.

113
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:54,920
That happens below the level of choice, and it is genuinely trying to help.

114
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:58,920
But the world it builds is invisible to you, and that is the whole problem.

115
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,920
You feel the verdict without ever seeing the courtroom.

116
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,920
So say it out loud.

117
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,920
When the drop comes, name the thing you are being compared to.

118
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,920
Not what is wrong with you, but what is this being measured against.

119
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,920
And then ask the only question that matters.

120
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,920
Is there a rematch? If there is, keep it.

121
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:22,920
That ache is Roese's preparation signal doing its job,

122
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:27,920
and it is pointing at a gap you can genuinely close, so let it.

123
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:34,920
But if there is no rematch, if the flight is gone, if the job went to someone else six months ago,

124
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:41,920
if the version of you being used as the ruler was never on offer, then the comparison has no work left to do.

125
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:46,920
It is not preparing you for anything. It is just standing in your kitchen charging you rent.

126
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:51,920
That is the moment you get to do what the bronze medalist does automatically.

127
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:57,920
Turn around. Look at the other side. Because there is always another invented world available,

128
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:03,920
the one where you did not get on the podium at all, and it is exactly as real as the one your mind picked for you.

129
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,920
Which is to say, not at all.

130
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:13,920
So tonight, just this. The athlete who came second was never unhappy about the medal around their neck.

131
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,920
They were unhappy about a medal hanging in a world that never happened.

132
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:22,920
And so are you, most nights, standing in your real life next to a person who does not exist,

133
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,920
losing to them quietly, over and over.

134
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:31,920
You do not have to win that race. You only have to look up and notice that the person beating you was never in the room.

135
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:35,920
And notice how easily you see all of this in other people.

136
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:41,920
When a friend is quietly wrecking themselves over a life that was never actually on offer, you can spot it in about four seconds.

137
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,920
Your own version of it stays completely invisible.

138
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:51,920
So one last question before you go. Are you the one your friends call at midnight, the person who says the calm,

139
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:56,920
right thing in ten minutes and always seems to know exactly what they should do?

140
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:03,920
And then your own turn comes, and you sit frozen for three weeks over a decision you could have solved for anybody else in a single sentence.

141
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:07,920
If that is you, you are not a hypocrite and you are not weak.

142
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,920
You are running a glitch three thousand years old, it has a name,

143
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:18,920
and there is one very small change in the way you speak to yourself that hands all of that wasted wisdom straight back to you.

144
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,920
That is the video on your screen right now.

145
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,920
Why you give great advice you never take yourself.

146
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:26,420
Watch that one next.
